var i=[{title:`Why screening matters`,text:`Most people benefit from being more active, and gentle movement is often a good place to start. Symptoms, unstable health conditions or recent medical events can change which activities are safe for you. Screening tells you when a health professional or qualified exercise professional should guide your next steps.`},{title:`The PAR-Q+`,text:`The current Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire for Everyone (PAR-Q+) uses structured health questions with follow-up pathways, so it goes further than a single generic checklist. Think about heart or cardiovascular conditions, uncontrolled blood pressure, fainting or unexplained dizziness, chest discomfort, recent surgery, new or worsening pain, and medications that change how your body responds to exercise. Pregnancy and the months after giving birth also call for individual advice.`},{title:`What a yes answer means`,text:`Answering yes to a screening question does not automatically mean no exercise. It may mean finishing the official follow-up process or getting advice about intensity and restrictions. If you already exercise safely, continuing at a familiar level is a different decision from starting hard training you have never done.`},{title:`Example`,text:`Someone who has been inactive for years and feels chest pressure during brisk walking should not try a maximal strength or interval session. They should stop that activity and get a medical assessment. Severe or ongoing chest symptoms need emergency care. Someone without worrying symptoms can start with easy walking and light strength practice, then build up gradually.`},{title:`Using TrainUp`,text:`You can record limitations by body area in Profile, with notes, and the plan picks only exercises that fit them. TrainUp cannot clear a medical condition or interpret your symptoms, and a generated workout is not medical approval. If screening raises concerns, ask your doctor before you choose a demanding plan.`}];var n=[{label:`Official PAR-Q+ screening`,url:`https://eparmedx.com/`},{label:`CDC getting started`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/healthy-weight-growth/physical-activity/getting-started.html`}];var a={sections:i,sources:n};export{a as default,i as sections,n as sources};